.TH uue 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
uue, uud \- encode a file as printable text, and decode it again
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uue
[\fB\-n\fR] \fIinputfile\fR [\fB\-\fR]
.br
.B uud
[\fB\-n\fR] [\fB\-d\fR] [\fB\-s\fI dir\fR] [\fB\-t\fI dir\fR] [\fIinputfile\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uue
writes
.IB inputfile .uue
holding the file as printable text, with the original name recorded
inside it.
.B uud
reads such a file and recreates the original under that name.
.PP
This is how files move over the serial console: the text can be pasted
into a terminal or captured from one, and needs no second port and no
protocol.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-n
Do not check or write the checksum.
.TP
.B \-
Write to standard output instead of to a file.
.TP
.B \-d
Delete the encoded file after decoding it.
.TP
.BI \-s " dir"
Look for the input in
.IR dir .
.TP
.BI \-t " dir"
Put the decoded file in
.IR dir .
.SH EXAMPLES
Sending a file to the board:
.nf
# cat > prog.uue
(paste the text, then the end-of-file key)
# uud prog.uue
.fi
.PP
Fetching one from it:
.nf
# uue report.txt
# cat report.uue
(capture the text in your terminal program)
.fi
.SH NOTES
The encoded form is about a third larger than the original, and goes
over the console at 115200 baud, so a 100K binary takes a couple of
minutes.
.PP
.B uud
takes the output name from inside the file, not from the command line.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rx (1),
.BR sx (1),
.BR tar (1),
.BR fat (1)
